title: LSP Diagnostics - (Example)

\procedure lsp.dg.greet(who) Hello <<who>>

Open this tiddler's `.tid` file in your editor and wait a moment for the warnings to settle. Only the first of these four lines is warned, as //No tiddler titled 'No Such Tiddler'//:

See [[No Such Tiddler]].

See [[LSP Server]].

Code `[[No Such Tiddler]]` here.

See [[docs|https://tiddlywiki.com]].

The second resolves. The third is inline code, which is not wikitext. The fourth is an external address, not a title.

Now add a link of your own to a title that does not exist, and watch the warning appear once you stop typing. Nothing needs saving: the server answers from the text in the editor.

!! Names nothing defines

These two calls name nothing the wiki defines, so each gets three grey dots under its name. Hover them for the message:

<<lsp.dg.gret>>

<$lisst filter="[tag[LSP]]"/>

Put the cursor on a name and press <kbd>Ctrl+.</kbd>: `lsp.dg.gret` is offered //Change to lsp.dg.greet//, defined at the top of this tiddler, and `$lisst` is offered //Change to $list//. Undo afterwards.

This call gets no dots, since ~JavaScript sets the variable for the actions of `<$radio>` and `<$range>`: <<actionValue>>

Nor do these, which ~JavaScript builds for the actions of `<$eventcatcher>` and `<$messagecatcher>`: <<dom-class>> <<event-param>>

In VS Code, run //~TiddlyWiki LSP: List undefined calls and widgets//. The Problems panel lists both names of this tiddler as information, and a click on one opens it here.

!! Filter steps that name no operator

`tagg` is no filter operator, so ~TiddlyWiki silently tests a field called `tagg` and this list stays empty. It gets the same grey dots, and <kbd>Ctrl+.</kbd> offers //Change to tag//:

<$list filter="[tagg[LSP]]"/>

A field test written on purpose gets no dots: `caption` is a field tiddlers have, and the core's table of contents tests `toc-link` itself:

<$list filter="[caption[LSP]] [toc-link[no]]"/>

